messages.content:
The function receives two values and returns their sum. The final line shows one test call.

```
function add1(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

console.log(add1(3, 7)); // 10
```

The * operator multiplies the two arguments. Returning the result lets other code reuse it.

```
function multiply1(a, b) {
  return a * b;
}

console.log(multiply1(3, 7)); // 21
```

The remainder operator % gives what is left after division. Even numbers have a remainder of zero when divided by two.

```
function isEven1(number) {
  return number % 2 === 0;
}

console.log(isEven1(3)); // false
```

The name is passed into the function as a parameter. The backticks allow the value to be inserted into the string.

```
function greet1(name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}

console.log(greet1('Nova'));
```

Math.max returns the largest argument. The spread operator ... passes each array item as a separate argument.

```
const numbers = [3, 7, 10];
const largest = Math.max(...numbers);
console.log(largest); // 10
```

reduce visits each item and carries a running total. The starting value is zero.

```
const numbers = [3, 7, 10];
const total = numbers.reduce((sum, number) => sum + number, 0);
console.log(total); // 20
```

filter creates a new array containing only values that pass the condition. The original array is unchanged.

```
const numbers = [2, 4, 10];
const result = numbers.filter(number => number > 3);
console.log(result);
```

First add all values, then divide by the number of values. For an empty array, add a check before dividing.

```
const numbers = [3, 7, 10];
const average = numbers.reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0) / numbers.length;
console.log(average); // 6.66667
```

There are 60 seconds in one minute, so multiply the input by 60.

```
function minutesToSeconds(minutes) {
  return minutes * 60;
}

console.log(minutesToSeconds(3)); // 180
```

toUpperCase() returns a new uppercase string; it does not change the original string.

```
const playerName = 'Nova';
const upperName = playerName.toUpperCase();
console.log(upperName); // NOVA
```

Flexbox centers the panel on both axes. The parent needs a height, such as min-height: 100vh, for vertical centering to be visible.

```
.game-screen {
  min-height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
```

Grid creates the columns and gap adds spacing. The media query changes the layout to one column on narrower screens.

```
.game-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 12px;
}

@media (max-width: 650px) {
  .game-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}
```

The :hover rule applies while a pointer is over the button. Keep a visible keyboard focus style as well.

```
.play-button {
  background: #2563eb;
  color: white;
  transition: background-color 150ms ease;
}

.play-button:hover {
  background: #1d4ed8;
}
```

The click listener runs after the user presses the button. Make sure both elements exist before this script runs, for example by using defer on the script tag.

```
const button = document.querySelector('#showPanel');
const panel = document.querySelector('#panel');

button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  panel.hidden = false;
});
```

The variable stores the count. Each click increases it and updates the page text.

```
let clicks = 0;
const button = document.querySelector('#clickButton');
const output = document.querySelector('#clickCount');

button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  clicks += 1;
  output.textContent = clicks;
});
```

The score is stored as a number. The function changes the value and then updates the displayed text.

```
let score = 0;
const scoreElement = document.querySelector('#score');

function addPoints() {
  score += 7;
  scoreElement.textContent = `Score: ${score}`;
}
```

createElement makes a new list item, textContent sets its text safely, and append adds it to the list.

```
const list = document.querySelector('#inventory');
const entry = document.createElement('li');
entry.textContent = 'coin';
list.append(entry);
```

Number converts numeric text into a number. Number.isFinite rejects values such as NaN and Infinity.

```
function isPositiveNumber(value) {
  const number = Number(value);
  return Number.isFinite(number) && number > 0;
}
```

arc creates a circle path. fillStyle sets its color, and fill paints the inside of the path.

```
const canvas = document.querySelector('#gameCanvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

ctx.beginPath();
ctx.arc(80, 80, 25, 0, Math.PI * 2);
ctx.fillStyle = 'orange';
ctx.fill();
```

The player object stores position and size. fillRect draws the rectangle using those values.

```
const canvas = document.querySelector('#gameCanvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
const player = { x: 40, y: 40, width: 30, height: 30 };

ctx.fillStyle = '#38bdf8';
ctx.fillRect(player.x, player.y, player.width, player.height);
```

The loop updates the game, draws a frame, and requests another frame. Define update and draw before this loop runs.

```
function gameLoop(time) {
  update(time);
  draw();
  requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
}

requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
```

The keys object records pressed state. The keyup listener resets it so movement stops when the key is released.

```
const keys = {};

window.addEventListener('keydown', event => {
  if (event.key === 'ArrowRight') keys.right = true;
});

window.addEventListener('keyup', event => {
  if (event.key === 'ArrowRight') keys.right = false;
});
```

dt is elapsed time in seconds. Multiplying speed by dt makes movement depend on time rather than frame count.

```
const player = { x: 0, speed: 180 };

function update(dt) {
  player.x += player.speed * dt;
}
```

The function checks for overlap on both the horizontal and vertical axes. It works for axis-aligned rectangles.

```
function overlaps(a, b) {
  return a.x < b.x + b.width &&
         a.x + a.width > b.x &&
         a.y < b.y + b.height &&
         a.y + a.height > b.y;
}
```

Pointer Events work with touch, mouse, and pen. Resetting the state on pointerup and pointercancel helps prevent a control from staying pressed.

```
const button = document.querySelector('#jumpButton');

button.addEventListener('pointerdown', () => {
  player.jumpRequested = true;
});

button.addEventListener('pointerup', () => {
  player.jumpRequested = false;
});

button.addEventListener('pointercancel', () => {
  player.jumpRequested = false;
});
```

