Why does this code throw an error?

let playerName = 'Nova';
console.log(playername);

playerName and playername are different identifiers because the capital N matters.

JavaScript variable names are case-sensitive. Use the same spelling:
```
let playerName = 'Nova';
console.log(playerName);
```

Why does this event handler say event is undefined?

button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log(event.target);
});

The event object should be declared as the listener's parameter instead of relying on a global variable.

Receive the event as a parameter:
```
button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  console.log(event.target);
});
```

Why can this comparison give an unexpected result?

const level = '10';
if (level > 2) console.log('high');

Form input and quoted values are strings. Convert them explicitly when numeric comparison is intended.

Convert the value to a number when it represents a number:
```
const level = Number('10');
if (level > 2) console.log('high');
```

Why does this function not use the supplied name?

function greet() {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}
greet('Nova');

The argument is passed by the caller, but the function needs a matching parameter to receive it.

Add a parameter and use it inside the function:
```
function greet(name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}
greet('Nova');
```

Why is the image missing when I draw it immediately?

const image = new Image();
image.src = 'player.png';
ctx.drawImage(image, 0, 0);

Image loading is asynchronous. Wait for the load event before drawing it.

Draw after the image has loaded:
```
const image = new Image();
image.onload = () => {
  ctx.drawImage(image, 0, 0);
};
image.src = 'player.png';
```

A pressed-key state must be reset when the key is released. In a real game, check event.key so unrelated keys do not trigger movement.

Also listen for keyup and clear the state:
```
window.addEventListener('keydown', () => keys.right = true);
window.addEventListener('keyup', () => keys.right = false);
```

