Add a parameter and use it inside the function:
```
function greet(name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}
greet('Echo');
```

|| means OR; && means AND. Choose the operator that matches the rule you want.

Use && when both conditions must be true:
```
if (score > 14 && health > 6) {
  startBonus();
}
```

Why does this check behave incorrectly?

let health = 15;
if (health = 0) {
  console.log('game over');
}

A single = assigns a value. Three equals signs compare values without type conversion.

Use strict equality to compare instead of assignment:
```
if (health === 0) {
  console.log('game over');
}
```

Why does this function give undefined?

function getScore() {
  const score = 15;
}
console.log(getScore());

A local variable is not automatically sent back to the caller. Use return.

Return the value from the function:
```
function getScore() {
  const score = 15;
  return score;
}
console.log(getScore());
```

Why does adding these values produce text instead of a sum?

const first = '15';
const second = '2';
console.log(first + second);

Why does this score update fail?

const score = 15;
score += 2;

A const binding cannot be reassigned. Use let for a value that changes.

Use let because the variable is reassigned:
```
let score = 15;
score += 2;
```

Why does this code throw an error?

let playerName = 'Bolt';
console.log(playername);

playerName and playername are different identifiers because the capital N matters.

JavaScript variable names are case-sensitive. Use the same spelling:
```
let playerName = 'Bolt';
console.log(playerName);
```

